{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Based on the standard implementation of a stable merge (which the provided text describes), here is the answer:\n\n**How many times does it write each position?**\nIt writes each output position **exactly once**.\n\n**How do the cursor loops guarantee that?**\nThe guarantee comes from the fact that the merge algorithm consumes elements from the two input sequences in a strictly linear fashion without backtracking. Specifically:\n\n1.  **Monotonic Progress:** Two cursors (or indices) track the current position in each input sequence. At each step, the algorithm compares the elements pointed to by the cursors and writes the smaller one (or the left one in case of a tie, to maintain stability) to the next available position in the output list.\n2.  **No Backtracking:** Once an element is written to the output, the corresponding cursor in the input sequence advances forward. The cursors never move backward.\n3.  **One-to-One Mapping:** Because the cursors only move forward and each step produces exactly one output element, every position in the output list is filled by a unique element from the inputs. No output position is written more than once, and no input element is skipped.\n\nIn summary, the merge is a linear process"}}],"created":1790853489,"model":"qwen38-fast-turbo4-mtp","system_fingerprint":"b14133-d18861064","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":256,"prompt_tokens":5659,"total_tokens":5915,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":5605}},"id":"chatcmpl-rbVnurHEZ3u11YQltFCusr0ozDIOUb5e","timings":{"cache_n":5605,"prompt_n":54,"prompt_ms":813.735,"prompt_per_token_ms":15.069166666666668,"prompt_per_second":66.36067024276944,"predicted_n":256,"predicted_ms":7545.895,"predicted_per_token_ms":29.59174509803922,"predicted_per_second":33.793208095262386,"draft_n":186,"draft_n_accepted":116,"kv_bpv":4.125},"mtp_request_counters":{"drafted":186,"accepted":116,"co